- **Bean Validation**: if the request has `@Valid`/constraints, assert a constraint violation comes back as a 400 with the expected error body. This is the one thing `@WebMvcTest` uniquely verifies and a plain unit test cannot.

## If the project doesn't already use slices

Match whatever the neighboring test classes do — read one or two first. If the codebase is uniformly `@SpringBootTest`, flag the narrower-slice option rather than silently introducing an inconsistent style. But by default, for a controller whose only job is to call a service, `@WebMvcTest` is the narrowest slice that covers what's under test, and it's the right default.

Save `@SpringBootTest` for when the thing under test genuinely is the wiring across the whole context — end-to-end configuration, cross-bean transactions, security filter chains spanning layers — not for exercising one controller's routing and serialization.
